update the pipeline 

with stages of docker build and scanning 


 stage('Docker Image Build') {
            steps {
                script {
                    def imageName = "906353/${env.APP_NAME}:${env.VERSION}"
                    sh "docker build -t ${imageName} ."
                    sh "docker images"
                }
            }
        }

stage('Trivy Image Scan') {
            steps {

                echo "Docker image scanning"
                
                sh "trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL 906353/${env.APP_NAME}:${env.VERSION}"

            }
        }


docker build → builds the image from your Dockerfile.

Tagging with ${env.BUILD_NUMBER} ensures each build has a unique version.


Then scan:

	trivy image myapp:latest


For HIGH and CRITICAL:

	trivy image \
	  --severity HIGH,CRITICAL \
	  myapp:latest

Fail the build for CRITICAL:

	trivy image \
	  --severity CRITICAL \
	  --exit-code 1 \
	  myapp:latest



8. Severity Levels

A common vulnerability classification is:

Severity		Meaning

UNKNOWN			Severity cannot be determined

LOW				Low impact

MEDIUM			Moderate risk

HIGH			Significant risk

CRITICAL		Very serious vulnerability

In production, a common policy might be:

LOW       	→ Inform
MEDIUM    	→ Inform
HIGH      	→ Review
CRITICAL  	→ Fail pipeline



stage('Trivy Image Scan') {
            steps {
                sh '''
                    trivy image \
                    --severity HIGH,CRITICAL \
                    --exit-code 1 \
                    myapp:${BUILD_NUMBER}
                '''
            }
        }



